WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.237 --> 00:00:02.350
(playful music)

00:00:02.350 --> 00:00:03.740
- [Narrator] Volcanic
corruptions like this

00:00:03.740 --> 00:00:07.920
may look mesmerizing and sometimes
attract flux of tourists,

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:09.410
but they're also dangerous events

00:00:09.410 --> 00:00:10.880
that impact human activities,

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:13.053
displace people, and cost lives.

00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:17.290
- The prediction is that we
did lose some 50% of our GDP

00:00:17.290 --> 00:00:19.990
as a result of the volcanic eruption.

00:00:19.990 --> 00:00:20.880
- [Narrator] Take this volcano

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:22.780
on the Caribbean island of St. Vincent.

00:00:22.780 --> 00:00:25.490
No deaths were reported, but
many people had to evacuate

00:00:25.490 --> 00:00:27.860
and ash blanketed entire neighborhoods.

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:30.660
Or this Icelandic volcano
that erupted in 2010,

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:32.020
paralyzing air traffic

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:35.660
and costing nearly $5
billion to global GDP.

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:37.800
So far, tools like seismicity monitors

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:40.180
have allowed volcanologists
to forecast unrest

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:42.150
days or weeks in advance,

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:45.370
but now NASA scientists say
they can help forecast eruptions

00:00:45.370 --> 00:00:48.650
months or even years ahead of
time using satellite imagery

00:00:48.650 --> 00:00:50.890
and monitoring changes
in ground temperature.

00:00:50.890 --> 00:00:52.430
- We wanted to understand

00:00:52.430 --> 00:00:56.070
to what extent the heat released
by volcanoes can be used

00:00:56.070 --> 00:00:58.800
to detect when volcanoes
start to reactivate.

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:01.918
There are many impacts
of volcanic eruptions

00:01:01.918 --> 00:01:04.473
and our goal is to minimize those impacts

00:01:04.473 --> 00:01:06.030
as much as possible.

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:07.630
- [Narrator] So here's how it works

00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:09.550
and how scientists say we could benefit

00:01:09.550 --> 00:01:10.823
from this new research.

00:01:12.430 --> 00:01:14.840
An early sign of a
volcanic eruption is heat,

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:16.510
as the magma underground rises,

00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:18.640
bringing warm gases to the surface.

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:21.490
- We see that these heat
emissions, they start to appear

00:01:21.490 --> 00:01:24.847
for several months to
years before eruption

00:01:24.847 --> 00:01:27.010
and we observe these signals prior

00:01:27.010 --> 00:01:30.720
to the changes that we observe
with other types of signals.

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:32.420
- [Narrator] The problem is
that these heat emissions

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:34.500
can be hard to monitor on the ground,

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:37.440
So Girona and his team turned to the sky.

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:39.370
NASA satellites have
already been recording

00:01:39.370 --> 00:01:41.620
years of information on thermal activity.

00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:45.140
- In fact, none of these
satellites have been designed

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:46.980
specifically to explore volcanoes,

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:48.464
but we can still use that information

00:01:48.464 --> 00:01:51.383
for monitoring volcanic eruptions.

00:01:51.383 --> 00:01:52.870
- [Narrator] After looking at two decades

00:01:52.870 --> 00:01:55.230
of radiant heat data
for several volcanoes,

00:01:55.230 --> 00:01:57.140
the researchers found a pattern.

00:01:57.140 --> 00:01:58.960
In the years leading up to interruption,

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:01.690
temperatures increased
over much of each volcano

00:02:01.690 --> 00:02:03.860
before dropping again after the eruption.

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:06.670
- [Tarsilo] We are detecting
the early reactivation

00:02:06.670 --> 00:02:08.750
of the volcano, which is crucial

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:10.963
to improve the monitoring efforts.

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:13.860
- [Narrator] But before this technology

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:15.860
can be fully deployed in the real world,

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:18.690
scientists will have to
process much more data.

00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:19.610
- Hopefully in a few years,

00:02:19.610 --> 00:02:21.750
we can have a better
understanding of the signals,

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:23.690
better understanding of volcanoes,

00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:27.440
and we can provide accurate forecasts.

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:30.690
- [Narrator] With about 1,500
active volcanoes on Earth

00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:33.470
and troves of satellite
data for each one of them,

00:02:33.470 --> 00:02:34.800
the NASA team is seeking help

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:37.603
from algorithms and other
researchers around the world.

00:02:38.450 --> 00:02:40.770
- This is another tool in our toolbox.

00:02:40.770 --> 00:02:41.960
- [Narrator] That's Mike Poland.

00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:45.010
He's a geophysicist for
the U.S. Geological Survey.

00:02:45.010 --> 00:02:47.510
He says that these satellite
images will have to be used

00:02:47.510 --> 00:02:49.950
in tandem with other traditional methods.

00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:50.970
- If you're building a house,

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:52.730
you don't just show up with a screwdriver.

00:02:52.730 --> 00:02:55.350
You've got a screwdriver, a hammer, a saw,

00:02:55.350 --> 00:02:57.240
and we need a diverse toolkit

00:02:57.240 --> 00:03:00.410
so that we can recognize all
the different types of signs

00:03:00.410 --> 00:03:02.670
that volcanoes might be giving us.

00:03:02.670 --> 00:03:04.330
- [Narrator] Seismometers gas, sensors,

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:06.550
and radar sensors are some of the tools

00:03:06.550 --> 00:03:08.863
that volcanologists will continue to use.

00:03:09.710 --> 00:03:11.640
Giving communities more time to prepare

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:14.723
could make the difference between
saving livelihoods or not.

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:19.280
In St. Vincent, the local
observatory noticed signs

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:21.070
of unrest weeks in advance

00:03:21.070 --> 00:03:24.010
and officials were ready to
evacuate 20,000 residents

00:03:24.010 --> 00:03:25.720
before the volcano erupted,

00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:28.380
helping to avoid casualties
and greater damage.

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:29.850
But with even more warning,

00:03:29.850 --> 00:03:32.210
officials say they may have
been able to avoid damage

00:03:32.210 --> 00:03:34.640
such as water contamination, for example.

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:36.960
- When the massive
eruption there took place,

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:39.400
it compromised our entire water supply.

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:42.850
All of our reservoirs were
contaminated with ash.

00:03:42.850 --> 00:03:44.720
We were without water for a couple of days

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:46.010
and perhaps going forward,

00:03:46.010 --> 00:03:49.463
we can look at maybe putting in tents

00:03:49.463 --> 00:03:52.680
where persons can store water properly.

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:55.311
We have to look at greater mitigation

00:03:55.311 --> 00:03:57.350
where natural disasters are concerned.

00:03:57.350 --> 00:04:00.230
- [Narrator] And in the case
of that Icelandic volcano,

00:04:00.230 --> 00:04:01.870
scientists say that more warning

00:04:01.870 --> 00:04:04.730
would have given the aviation
industry more time to adapt

00:04:04.730 --> 00:04:06.240
and find alternative routes.

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:07.710
- It's important to forecast eruptions

00:04:07.710 --> 00:04:11.350
because we can reduce the
impact at a local scale,

00:04:11.350 --> 00:04:13.650
but also at a global scale

00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:16.484
if you're talking about
much larger eruptions.

00:04:16.484 --> 00:04:20.151
(tranquil futuristic music)

